    "name":  "Primary lung cancer",
    "module":  "oncology",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Primary lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related mortality worldwide. The two major histological categories are non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC, ~85%) — including adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and large cell carcinoma — and small cell lung cancer (SCLC, ~15%). Most cases are attributable to tobacco smoking. Central tumours (squamous, SCLC) tend to present with cough, haemoptysis, and obstructive features. Peripheral tumours (adenocarcinoma) may be silent until advanced. Paraneoplastic syndromes (SIADH, Cushing, Lambert-Eaton) occur particularly with SCLC.",
